From 38483ec12024ed3e9b4b7b0f58ecf678db1e941e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Eden (Doris) build" <doris@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 00:53:44 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 51/51] Eden (Doris): GPU-drawn textures get only the blocks
 the game changed uploaded; same for GPU engine writes done on the CPU;
 separate heap rebuilds logged

- The build 19 log named the textures that lost what the GPU drew: one family, R8G8 260x260
  with 9 mip levels (256 + 2-pixel borders: terrain tiles) and the render targets of its levels,
  uploaded again from guest memory up to 141 times in 10 s. Eden uploads a whole texture again
  when the game's code changes any part of it; everything else the GPU drew in it (other levels,
  borders, the rest of the level) was replaced with old contents.
- A game write to a GPU-drawn texture (NCE write fault) now keeps a copy of the texture's bytes
  on up to 64 pages per texture (32 MiB in all); at the next use or the end of the frame the
  512-byte blocks whose bytes changed are found and only they are uploaded (plain 2D textures,
  arrays and mip levels included; other layouts, or more pages, are uploaded whole as in Eden).
  An alias's changed blocks are uploaded before it is copied into the texture.
- DMA copies and inline uploads Eden does on the CPU (already in guest memory when the texture
  cache hears of them) upload just the written blocks of GPU-drawn textures right away.
- VideoCommon::DorisGobCopies maps byte ranges to block rectangles per level and layer; checked
  on the host against UnswizzleImage for 11 layouts (every block, and 300 random range sets each).
- Report: partial uploads, writes over too many pages, which path marked a GPU-drawn texture as
  changed as a whole, separate heap mappings; every separate heap rebuild is logged with its time.

